  "query_preview": "..\n\nA deep upper low is advancing across Lower MI this morning with an \nimpressive presentation to the closed circulation noted in satellite \nWV imagery. Fgen associated with the aggressive height falls and PVA \nexits to the east over the next hour with a surge of dry air aloft \nquickly working in from the northwest. This brings an end to the \nlight to moderate rainfall, with a lull in precip until mid morning \nwhen deeper moisture sweeps back across the area. Cool northwest \nwind with pockets of lake enhanced light rain and/or drizzle bring a \nraw, grungy day with temperatures to struggling to reach 50 degrees \nthis afternoon.\n\nAs the deep cyclonic circulation passes overhead, a surface mesolow \nis forecast to drop across Lake Huron this afternoon and evening. \nThis brings a strongly forced mesoscale band of rain within the \ntrowal/deformation axis into the vicinity of the eastern Thumb. \nGiven the scale of forcing, placement of the heaviest rain carries \nuncertainty but confidence has increased for rainfall totals in \nexcess of 0.50\" from Port Austin to Port Hope and Harbor Beach. A \nsmall handful of hi-res solutions and individual members of ENS and \nGEPS ensembles indicate potential for localized areas to receive \nover 1 inch in the event the mesolow is slower to pass into the \nOntario peninsula tonight. Surface temps in the 40s and wet bulb \nzero heights as low as 2000 ft offer high confidence in rain as the \nptype.\n\nLight rain may linger into Saturday morning along the Thumb \nshoreline, otherwise negative vorticity advection ensues in the wake \nof the departing upper low to cease precip chances. Moist cyclonic \nflow below 800mb keeps a good amount of clouds around Saturday and \nthermal troughing helps hold high temps in the lower 50s. Shortwave \nridging crests over the area Saturday night before the next trough \narrives from the northwest on Sunday. This wave is also deep but \nmore progressive, targeting Lake Huron with its main height fall \ncorridor. Light rain may brush the Thumb, but the main impact \nlocally will be a breezy westerly wind as the system passes to the \nnorth. Milder conditions develop Sunday into Tuesday as positive \nupper height anomalies prevail over the upper Midwest and Great \nLakes. This period is generally devoid of organized forcing, \nfavoring dry conditions.",
